; 34-prefix.kurt

; `prefix OP rbp` declares `OP` as a prefix operator (applied in front of its
; single argument) with a right binding power controlling how much it grabs.

prefix "-" 100

var a
parse -a               ; (- a)

; Space (function application, built into `minimal.kurt`) already lets you
; write `f a` for "f applied to a" -- `prefix` is for when you want a
; *different* binding power than space's, e.g. so that `- a + b` parses as
; `(- a) + b` rather than `- (a + b)` (see `doc/kurt-doc.md`, "difference
; between prefix and function").
